body:
### What happens?

With nested schemas, `COMMENT ON TABLE`, `COMMENT ON VIEW` and `COMMENT ON COLUMN` fail with "Too many qualifications found" as soon as the name has more than three entries (more than four for a column), while `SELECT`, `CREATE TABLE`, `ALTER TABLE` and `CREATE VIEW` accept the same longer paths. A table in `sales.europe` can be commented only without its catalog name, and a table in `sales.europe.north` cannot be commented at all.

### To Reproduce

In the CLI, started without a database file (so the catalog is named `memory`):

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA sales;
CREATE SCHEMA sales.europe;
CREATE SCHEMA sales.europe.north;
CREATE TABLE sales.europe.orders (id INTEGER);
CREATE TABLE sales.europe.north.deep (id INTEGER);
CREATE VIEW sales.europe.v AS SELECT 1 AS id;

-- these succeed
COMMENT ON TABLE sales.europe.orders IS 'x';
COMMENT ON COLUMN sales.europe.orders.id IS 'x';
COMMENT ON VIEW sales.europe.v IS 'x';

-- these fail
COMMENT ON TABLE memory.sales.europe.orders IS 'x';
COMMENT ON TABLE sales.europe.north.deep IS 'x';
COMMENT ON COLUMN sales.europe.north.deep.id IS 'x';
COMMENT ON VIEW memory.sales.europe.v IS 'x';
```

Each failing statement returns:

```
Parser Error: Too many qualifications found - expected [catalog.schema.name] or [schema.name]
```

The error is raised while parsing, so it also appears for names that do not exist, for example `COMMENT ON TABLE a.b.c.d IS 'x'`. I found no workaround for `sales.europe.north.deep`: `USE sales.europe.north` and `SET search_path = 'sales.europe.north'` are refused, so the unqualified name does not resolve either.

The same longer paths work in other statements:

```sql
SELECT * FROM memory.sales.europe.north.deep;
ALTER TABLE memory.sales.europe.north.deep ADD COLUMN extra INTEGER;
CREATE VIEW memory.sales.europe.north.v AS SELECT 1 AS id;
```

In an attached database, a table in `a.b` can be commented only after `USE`, and a table in `a.b.c` cannot be commented at all:

```sql
ATTACH ':memory:' AS other;
CREATE SCHEMA other.a;
CREATE SCHEMA other.a.b;
CREATE SCHEMA other.a.b.c;
CREATE TABLE other.a.b.t (id INTEGER);
CREATE TABLE other.a.b.c.u (id INTEGER);

COMMENT ON TABLE other.a.b.t IS 'x';  -- fails
USE other;
COMMENT ON TABLE a.b.t IS 'x';        -- succeeds
COMMENT ON TABLE a.b.c.u IS 'x';      -- fails
```

### Expected

`COMMENT ON` accepts the same qualified paths as `CREATE TABLE` and `SELECT`: the catalog plus any number of schema levels, for tables, views and columns. The [documentation for nested schemas](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/sql/statements/create_schema#nested-schemas) says objects in a nested schema are referenced by their fully qualified, dot-separated path, and the [limitations of `COMMENT ON`](https://duckdb.org/docs/2.0/sql/statements/comment_on#limitations) do not mention nested schemas.

### Where the limit is

`TransformCommentStatement` (`src/parser/peg/transformer/transform_comment.cpp`) passes the dotted name to `PEGTransformerFactory::StringToQualifiedName` (`src/parser/peg/transformer/peg_transformer_factory.cpp`), which throws this error for more than three entries. For `COMMENT ON COLUMN` the column name is split off first, so a column reference can have four. I have not built `main`, but this code is unchanged there (c15a232a57). #25070 removed 3- and 4-level limits elsewhere in the parser but did not touch `comment.gram` or `transform_comment.cpp`. The tests in `test/sql/catalog/nested_schema/` do cover `COMMENT ON` in nested schemas, but only with one level of nesting and no catalog prefix, for example `COMMENT ON TABLE s1.s2.t`. `test/sql/catalog/comment_on_extended.test` expects `COMMENT ON COLUMN galaxy.db.schema.table.col1 IS 'no bueno'` to fail, so a fix needs to change that test.

### Environment

- OS: macOS 26.7.1, arm64
- DuckDB version: v2.0.0 (source id d54cbbe3df)
- DuckDB client: CLI from the v2.0.0 release (`v2.0.0 (Cyanoptera) d54cbbe3df`) and R (duckdb package 2.0.0), with the same result in both
- Full name: Travis Gerke
- No data set is needed.

### What happens?

In MySQL
```sql
mysql> -- It "[-0]"
mysql> SELECT t4.c1 FROM t4 NATURAL  JOIN t0 WHERE -1828315342;
+--------------------+
| c1                 |
+--------------------+
| 0.2941334377345298 |
|                  0 |
|                 -0 |
+--------------------+
3 rows in set (0.00 sec)

mysql> -- It "[0]"
mysql> (SELECT t4.c1 FROM t4 NATURAL LEFT JOIN t0 WHERE -1828315342) EXCEPT ALL ((SELECT t4.c1 FROM t4 NATURAL LEFT JOIN t0 WHERE -1828315342) EXCEPT ALL (SELECT t4.c1 FROM t4 NATURAL RIGHT JOIN t0 WHERE -1828315342));
+--------------------+
| c1                 |
+--------------------+
| 0.2941334377345298 |
|                  0 |
|                 -0 |
+--------------------+
3 rows in set (0.00 sec)

mysql> select version();
+-----------+
| version() |
+-----------+
| 8.0.36    |
+-----------+
1 row in set (0.00 sec)
```

In DuckDB, The second equivalent query did not return -0, but instead returned 0.
```sql
D SELECT t4.c1 FROM t4 NATURAL  JOIN t0 WHERE -1828315342;
┌────────────────────┐
│         c1         │
│       double       │
├────────────────────┤
│ 0.2941334377345298 │
│                0.0 │
│               -0.0 │
└────────────────────┘
D (SELECT t4.c1 FROM t4 NATURAL LEFT JOIN t0 WHERE -1828315342) EXCEPT ALL ((SELECT t4.c1 FROM t4 NATURAL LEFT JOIN t0 WHERE -1828315342) EXCEPT ALL (SELECT t4.c1 FROM t4 NATURAL RIGHT JOIN t0 WHERE -1828315342));
┌────────────────────┐
│         c1         │
│       double       │
├────────────────────┤
│ 0.2941334377345298 │
│                0.0 │
│                0.0 │
└────────────────────┘
```
