body:
### What happens?

Any aggregate function registered through the C API
(`duckdb_create_aggregate_function` + `duckdb_register_aggregate_function`)
crashes the process when used as

- a window aggregate over a whole-partition frame, e.g. `agg(x) OVER ()`, or
- an ordered aggregate, e.g. `agg(x ORDER BY y)`.

Plain `SELECT agg(x) FROM t` and `GROUP BY` are fine.

Reproduced on the official Linux x86-64 `libduckdb` releases **v1.4.4, v1.5.0
and v1.5.5**, and still present in the source on `main` (checked at
`94d7b64`; not built and run there).

## Cause

`CAPIAggregateUpdate` (`src/main/capi/aggregate_function-c.cpp` in v1.5.5,
`src/main/capi/v1/aggregate_function-c.cpp` on `main`) flattens
the *input* vectors but reads the state vector as flat without flattening it
(v1.5.5 shown; `main` is the same apart from `GetDataMutableUnsafe`):

```cpp
auto state_data = FlatVector::GetDataUnsafe<duckdb_aggregate_state>(state);
...
bind_data.info.update(c_function_info, c_input_chunk, state_data);
```

(`CAPIAggregateCombine` and `CAPIAggregateFinalize` both flatten the state first —
`state.Flatten(count)` in v1.5.5, `state.Flatten()` on `main`; update is the odd
one out.)

The C API constructs its `AggregateFunction` with no fast-path update callback
(`simple_update` in v1.5.5, `cluster_update` on `main`), so two executors fall back
to calling `update` with a **constant** state vector and `count > 1`:

- `WindowConstantAggregatorLocalState` — `statep(Value::POINTER(0))`, then
  `state_p_data[0] = ...; GetStateUpdateCallback()(..., statep, count)`
  (`src/function/window/window_constant_aggregator.cpp`).
- `SortedAggregateFunction` — `agg_state_vec.SetVectorType(VectorType::CONSTANT_VECTOR);
  update(..., agg_state_vec, sliced.size())`
  (`src/function/aggregate/sorted_aggregate_function.cpp`).

The C callback receives `duckdb_aggregate_state *states` and, per the documented
contract, reads `states[i]` for every input row `i`. For a constant vector only
`states[0]` exists; `states[1..count)` is read past the end of the vector's buffer.

## Suggested fix

Either of:

1. In `CAPIAggregateUpdate`, when `state` is not a flat vector, hand the callback a
   **local** flattened copy (e.g. build a `count`-long array of the single state
   pointer from a `UnifiedVectorFormat`, or flatten a copy of the vector), rather than
   `state.Flatten(count)` in place. In-place flattening looks like the obvious
   one-liner, matching combine and finalize, but it is not safe for the window
   caller: `WindowConstantAggregatorLocalState::Sink` caches a pointer to
   `statep`'s data once (`FlatVector::GetData`, line 233 in v1.5.5;
   `ConstantVector::GetData`, line 205 on `main`) and writes `state_p_data[0]`
   on every later iteration (line 297 / 274). Flattening `statep` replaces its
   buffer, which would leave that cached pointer dangling.
2. Give C API aggregates the fast-path callback (`cluster_update` on `main`,
   `simple_update` in v1.5.5) that forwards to the C callback with a
   `count`-long array filled with the single state pointer. Both executors already
   prefer it when present.

(Neither fix has been built and tested here; the analysis is from the v1.5.5
source, re-checked against `main` at `94d7b64`.)

There is no way to work around this from the extension side: the callback gets a raw
`duckdb_aggregate_state *` and cannot tell a constant vector from a flat one, and no
registration option avoids these two executors.


### To Reproduce

Build against a `libduckdb` release (Linux x86-64 shown):

```
gcc repro.c -I<libduckdb dir> -L<libduckdb dir> -lduckdb -Wl,-rpath,<libduckdb dir> -o repro
```

The reproducer is `repro.c` below. The aggregate is a textbook sum:

```
$ ./repro group
SELECT capi_sum(i) FROM range(3000) t(i)
  -> 4498500
$ ./repro window     # SELECT capi_sum(i) OVER () FROM range(3000) t(i) LIMIT 1
Segmentation fault (exit 139)
$ ./repro ordered    # SELECT capi_sum(i ORDER BY i DESC) FROM range(3000) t(i)
Segmentation fault (exit 139)
```

Under AddressSanitizer (`gcc -fsanitize=address`, v1.5.5):

```
ERROR: AddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address 0x000000000000
  #0 update repro.c:17
  #1 duckdb::CAPIAggregateUpdate(...)
  #2 duckdb::WindowConstantAggregatorLocalState::Sink(...)
```

and for the ordered case

```
  #0 update repro.c:17
  #1 duckdb::CAPIAggregateUpdate(...)
  #2 duckdb::(anonymous namespace)::SortedAggregateFunction::Finalize(...)
```

`repro.c`:

```c
// Minimal C API reproducer: CAPIAggregateUpdate passes a CONSTANT state
// vector with count > 1 for `agg(x) OVER ()` and `agg(x ORDER BY y)`.
#include "duckdb.h"
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct { int64_t sum; } state_t;

static idx_t state_size(duckdb_function_info info) { return sizeof(state_t); }
static void state_init(duckdb_function_info info, duckdb_aggregate_state s) { ((state_t *)s)->sum = 0; }

static void update(duckdb_function_info info, duckdb_data_chunk input, duckdb_aggregate_state *states) {
	idx_t n = duckdb_data_chunk_get_size(input);
	int64_t *x = (int64_t *)duckdb_vector_get_data(duckdb_data_chunk_get_vector(input, 0));
	for (idx_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
		((state_t *)states[i])->sum += x[i]; // states[i] for i >= 1 is out of bounds when the vector is constant
	}
}
static void combine(duckdb_function_info info, duckdb_aggregate_state *src, duckdb_aggregate_state *dst, idx_t n) {
	for (idx_t i = 0; i < n; i++) ((state_t *)dst[i])->sum += ((state_t *)src[i])->sum;
}
static void finalize(duckdb_function_info info, duckdb_aggregate_state *src, duckdb_vector result, idx_t n, idx_t off) {
	int64_t *out = (int64_t *)duckdb_vector_get_data(result);
	for (idx_t i = 0; i < n; i++) out[off + i] = ((state_t *)src[i])->sum;
}

static void run(duckdb_connection con, const char *sql) {
	duckdb_result r;
	if (duckdb_query(con, sql, &r) == DuckDBError) {
		printf("%s\n  -> error: %s\n", sql, duckdb_result_error(&r));
	} else {
		duckdb_data_chunk c = duckdb_fetch_chunk(r);
		int64_t v = ((int64_t *)duckdb_vector_get_data(duckdb_data_chunk_get_vector(c, 0)))[0];
		printf("%s\n  -> %lld\n", sql, (long long)v);
		duckdb_destroy_data_chunk(&c);
	}
	duckdb_destroy_result(&r);
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
	duckdb_database db; duckdb_connection con;
	duckdb_open(NULL, &db); duckdb_connect(db, &con);
	duckdb_aggregate_function f = duckdb_create_aggregate_function();
	duckdb_aggregate_function_set_name(f, "capi_sum");
	duckdb_logical_type t = duckdb_create_logical_type(DUCKDB_TYPE_BIGINT);
	duckdb_aggregate_function_add_parameter(f, t);
	duckdb_aggregate_function_set_return_type(f, t);
	duckdb_destroy_logical_type(&t);
	duckdb_aggregate_function_set_functions(f, state_size, state_init, update, combine, finalize);
	if (duckdb_register_aggregate_function(con, f) != DuckDBSuccess) { puts("register failed"); return 1; }
	duckdb_destroy_aggregate_function(&f);
	const char *which = argc > 1 ? argv[1] : "all";
	if (!strcmp(which, "all") || !strcmp(which, "group"))
		run(con, "SELECT capi_sum(i) FROM range(3000) t(i)");                       /* control: 4498500 */
	if (!strcmp(which, "all") || !strcmp(which, "window"))
		run(con, "SELECT capi_sum(i) OVER () FROM range(3000) t(i) LIMIT 1");       /* expected 4498500 */
	if (!strcmp(which, "all") || !strcmp(which, "ordered"))
		run(con, "SELECT capi_sum(i ORDER BY i DESC) FROM range(3000) t(i)");       /* expected 4498500 */
	duckdb_disconnect(&con); duckdb_close(&db);
	return 0;
}
```


